* Secondary 91A60\n  * 00A08\n\n## ORCID\n\nAndrzej Komisarski\n\n### Acknowledgments\n\nI would like to express my profound gratitude to the editors and anonymous reviewers. They encouraged me to make several modifications to the text. In my opinion, the final version of the article is much better compared to its initial form.\n\n## To cite this article:\n\nReference style: [Input]APA [Input]Chicago [Input]Harvard\n\nCitation copied to clipboard\n\nCopy citation to clipboard\n\nReference styles above use APA (6th edition), Chicago (16th edition) & Harvard (10th edition)\n\n### Download citation\n\nDownload a citation file in RIS format that can be imported by citation management software including EndNote, ProCite, RefWorks and Reference Manager.\n\nChoose format: [Input]RIS [Input]BibTex [Input]RefWorks Direct Export\n\nChoose options: [Input]Citation [Input]Citation & abstract [Input]Citation & references\n\nDownload citations--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nScientific American, October, 1974 (https://mathwo.github.io/assets/files/penney_game/1974-10-01_Scientific_American.pdf)\nciteturn9search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.4 years ago; Crawled: 17.4 years ago; by Martin Gardner ... Consider the children’s game in which, on the count of three, one either makes a fist to symbolize “rock,” extends two fingers for “scissors” or all fingers for “paper.” ... Occasionally in mathematics, particularly in probability theory and decision theory, one comes on a relation that one expects to be transitive but actually is not.If the nontransitivity is so counter-intuitive as to boggle the mind, we have what is called a nontransitive paradox. ... Most of the early advocates of proportional representation were totally unaware of this Achilles’ heel; indeed, the paradox was not fully recognized by political theorists until the mid-1940’s, when Duncan Black, a Welsh economist, rediscovered it in connection with his monumental work on committee decision making. ... © 1974 SCIENTIFIC AMERICAN, INC\nMATHEMATICAL GAMES\n\n*On the paradoxical situations that arise from nontransitive relations*\n\nby Martin Gardner\n\nWhenever a relation R that applies to xRy and yRz also applies to xRz, the relation is said to be transitive. For example, “less than” is transitive among all real numbers. If 2 is less than π and the square root of 3 is less than 2, we can be certain that the square root of 3 is less than π. Equally also is transitive: if a = b and b = c, then a = c. In everyday life such relations as “earlier than,” “heavier than,” “taller than,” “inside of” and hundreds of others are transitive.\n\nIt is easy to think of relations that are not transitive. If A is the father of B and B is the father of C, it is never true that A is the father of C. If A loves B and B loves C, it does not follow that A loves C. Familiar games abound in transitive rules (if poker hand A beats B and B beats C, then A beats C), but some games have nontransitive (or intransitive) rules. Consider the children’s game in which, on the count of three, one either makes a fist to symbolize “rock,” extends two fingers for “scissors” or all fingers for “paper.” --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPossible Probability and Irreducibility of Balanced Nontransitive Dice - Hur - 2021 - Journal of Mathematics - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2021/6648248)\nciteturn9search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.7 years ago; Crawled: last month;   * 1 Gardner M., Mathematical games, Scientific American. (1970) 223, no. 6, 110–115, https://doi.org/10.1038/scientificamerican1270-110. ...   * 2 Gardner M., Mathematical games, Scientific American. (1974) 230, no. 5, 120–125, https://doi.org/10.1038/scientificamerican0574-120.\n## Conflicts of Interest\n\nThe authors declare that they have no conflicts of interest.\n\n## Acknowledgments\n\nThe first author was supported by Basic Science Research Program through the National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) funded by the Korea government (NRF-2019R1F1A1061300). The second author was partially supported by Chonnam National University (Grant no. 2018-0978) and by the National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) grant funded by the Korea government (MSIP) (no. 2017R1C1B2010081). The authors would like to thank the referees for a number of corrections and very useful suggestions.\n\n## Data Availability\n\nNo data were used to support this study.\n\n## References\n\n  * 1 Gardner M., Mathematical games, Scientific American. (1970) 223, no. 6, 110–115, https://doi.org/10.1038/scientificamerican1270-110.\n\nWeb of Science® Google Scholar\n\n  * 2 Gardner M., Mathematical games, Scientific American. (1974) 230, no. 5, 120–125, https://doi.org/10.1038/scientificamerican0574-120.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMartin Gardner and Scientific American (https://www.martin-gardner.org/SciAm1.html)\nciteturn9search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Every article in every issue of Scientific American from the 1970s and 1980s is indexed (and linked to) here: 1970s, and 1980s.John Miller's chronological list of every article Martin wrote for Scientific American, originally posted online with Martin's permission, is one of several helpful webpages for keeping track of these columns.\n\nThanks to the MAA, seven classic \"Mathematical Games\" columns are available online, as they appeared (retitled) in later books.\n\nEvery article in every issue of Scientific American from the 1970s and 1980s is indexed (and linked to) here: 1970s, and 1980s.\n\nJohn Miller's chronological list of every article Martin wrote for Scientific American, originally posted online with Martin's permission, is one of several helpful webpages for keeping track of these columns.\n\nIn Aug 1998, Martin wrote one final piece for Scientific American, \"A Quarter Century of Recreational Mathematics.\" In part, it was a survey of the best of the \"Mathematical Games\" columns, but he found ways to sneak in new challenges and results too. This delightful article, which he wrote at the age of 83, shows that he certainly hadn't lost his touch.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLet 'em roll (https://plus.maths.org/issue41/features/hobbs/2pdf/index.html/op.pdf)\nciteturn9search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Looking at a wins/losses table, the non−transitive nature of these dice can easily be seen: ... Efron also designed three sets of four die which are also non−transitive.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNon-transitive Dice (https://www.cut-the-knot.org/Probability/NonTransitiveDice.shtml)\nciteturn9search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; A set of four nontransitive dice has been invented by Bradley Efron and described by Martin Gardner [Wheels, Time Travel, Mathematical Games, The Colossal Book] in the early 1970s in his Scientific American column. ... In addition to Eforn's 4-dice, you'll find there 3- and 7- nontransitive dice sets.)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈PROBABILITY≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈≈ (https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~wgreene/Statistics/ProbabilityCollection.pdf)\nciteturn9search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; ))))))))))))))))EFRON’S DICE**************** ... This shows that the ordering of the dice is nontransitive.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThree Dice, Each Beaten by the Next - Mathomaly (https://mathomaly.com/explore/intransitive-dice/)\nciteturn9search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; The idea of nontransitive dice is old and is credited in the literature to Bradley Efron; the set on this page is not Efron’s.\n-----------------------------------------